---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import conformance from "../../data/conformance.json";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Implementations"
  description="Compare exact mdbase v0.3 conformance profiles and evidence for the Rust and TypeScript implementations."
  current="implementations"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero__inner">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow"><i></i> Generated from conformance claims</p>
        <h1>Implementation claims, without shorthand.</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="page-hero__aside">
        <p>
          mdbase v0.3 uses atomic profiles. Each row below names an artifact,
          version, evidence date, and the exact profiles it claims. Legacy
          “Level 6” descriptions do not apply to v0.3.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="/spec/#section-16">Conformance rules</a>
          <a class="button" href="https://github.com/mdbase-dev/mdbase-spec/tree/main/tests/v0.3">
            Shared fixtures
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Published artifacts</h2>
    <table class="profile-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Artifact</th>
          <th>Version</th>
          <th>Profiles</th>
          <th>Evidence</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          conformance.implementations.map((implementation) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <a href={implementation.repository}>{implementation.name}</a><br />
                <small>{implementation.language}</small>
              </td>
              <td><code>{implementation.version}</code></td>
              <td>
                {implementation.profiles.map((profile) => <code style="display: block">{profile}</code>)}
                {
                  implementation.optional_features.length > 0 && (
                    <small>Optional: {implementation.optional_features.join(", ")}</small>
                  )
                }
              </td>
              <td>
                <span>{implementation.evidence_date}</span><br />
                <small>{implementation.note}</small>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Fixture coverage status</h2>
    <p>
      An implementation claim and the shared suite's coverage status answer
      different questions. The claim says what an artifact asserts it passes.
      The coverage ledger says whether the specification's maintainers consider
      the shared fixture set complete for that profile.
    </p>
    <table class="profile-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Profile</th>
          <th>Shared fixture coverage</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          conformance.coverage.coverage_complete.map((profile) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{profile}</code></td>
              <td><span class="status">Coverage complete</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        {
          conformance.coverage.draft.map((profile) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{profile}</code></td>
              <td><span class="status status--draft">Draft coverage</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Workflow execution is intentionally not claimed yet.</strong>
      <p>
        The Rust runtime has durable workflow tests, including recovery and
        timers. Its current public conformance claim still records
        <code>runtime_execution: false</code>. The site will publish
        <code>workflow/0.1</code> only after the artifact has a separate claim
        backed by the shared execution fixture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

